.el .ds Aq '
.TH conduct-inspect 1  "conduct-inspect "
.SH NAME
conduct\-inspect \- Validate and describe one artifact without executing it
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBconduct inspect\fR [\fB\-\-type\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIARTIFACT\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Validate and describe one artifact without executing it
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-type\fR \fI<TYPE>\fR [default: auto]
Select a frozen artifact kind, or use marker\-only detection
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
panel
.IP \(bu 2
lowered\-source
.IP \(bu 2
execution\-plan
.IP \(bu 2
evidence
.IP \(bu 2
diagnostic
.IP \(bu 2
conformance
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIARTIFACT\fR>
Read the artifact from this file, or `\-` for stdin
.SH SAFETY
Inspection is read-only, bounded, marker-based, and non-executing.
It performs no network access, provider discovery, secret resolution, authority acquisition, dynamic loading, or artifact execution.
.SH STREAMS
Human or conduit.result/v1 inspection results are written to stdout.
Human or versioned JSON diagnostics are written to stderr.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates successful validation or normal downstream pipe closure.
Two indicates a command, input, validation, limit, or non-broken output failure.
